  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nGlobal models are coming into somewhat better agreement with the \npattern evolution this weekend. Both the ECMWF and GFS show high \npressure strengthening over the southern Plains with the western \nperiphery of the ridge extending over E AZ and NM. This will cause \nthe subtropical jet to shift wwd beginning Saturday, setting up \nbetter moisture advection over southeast CA and southwest AZ. NBM \nPoPs are still not overly impressive, mainly in the 20-30% range on \nSaturday, however the GEFS/GFS indicates a final slug of moisture \nfrom Marie being pulled into southeast CA on Sunday with PWATs \nrising above 2.0\". Therefore we will have to monitor for heavier \nrainfall potential in portions of SE California including Joshua \nTree NP on Sunday. Elsewhere, across southcentral AZ, rain chances \nlook to be meager due to more extensive cloud cover and very low \ninstability. The latest WPC QPF field shows a local minimum in \nrainfall totals (<0.10\") in southcentral AZ including the Phoenix \nMetro through this weekend. By early next week, there are large \ndiscrepancies in the ridge/trough positioning which will complicate \nthe precipitation forecast, but there will still be adequate \nmoisture across our region which will keep low end PoPs in place \nacross eastern and southeastern AZ Monday and Tuesday with \npotentially better precipitation chances arriving Wednesday.\n\nThe temperature forecast this weekend into early next week remains \nlargely unchanged, although there will likely be a gradual increase \nfrom near normal temperatures on Saturday to slightly above normal \ntemperatures Sunday into early next week as ridging strengthens over \nthe region. Depending on how strong the ridge gets, NBM temps could \nclimb a few more degrees in subsequent runs and it is not out of the \nquestion that we could see lower desert highs approaching 110 \ndegrees as indicated by the NBM 75th percentile on Monday and \nTuesday.",
